607c90dcf854f76638c5f29854ef14ecbac"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id [:question/math-academy-id "q-73178"] :question/problem "Find the function $f(x)$ and the value of $b,$ given that ${\\int }_{-\\pi /4}^{b}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{l=0}^{n-1}[{sec}^{2}⁡(-\\frac{\\pi }{4}+\\frac{\\pi l}{2n})\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{2n}].$" :question/worked-solution "A definite integral can be defined as the limit of a left Riemann sum as follows:\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{a}^{b}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{l=0}^{n-1}f({x}_{l})\\cdot Δx\n$$\n\nwhere $Δx=\\frac{b-a}{n}$ and ${x}_{l}=a+lΔx.$\n\nNotice that we're given that the lower limit $a=-\\frac{\\pi }{4}.$ \n\nComparing the given summation with the general expression above, we see that $Δx=\\frac{\\pi }{2n}.$ Therefore, \n\n$$\n\\frac{\\pi }{2n}=\\frac{(b-(-\\frac{\\pi }{4}))}{n}\\,⟹\\,b=\\frac{\\pi }{4}.\n$$\n\nConsequently, ${x}_{l}=-\\frac{\\pi }{4}+\\frac{\\pi l}{2n}$ and $f({x}_{l})={sec}^{2}⁡{x}_{l}.$\n\nHence,\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-\\pi /4}^{\\pi /4}{sec}^{2}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{l=0}^{n-1}[{sec}^{2}⁡(-\\frac{\\pi }{4}+\\frac{\\pi l}{2n})\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{2n}].\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is $f(x)={sec}^{2}⁡x$ and $b=\\frac{\\pi }{4}.$" :question/answer-fields ["q-73178/selection/ma-v1/cae4b3cbfe9c6697fc46cef3bdaadf104b73f94653a57c2d2c8c034ada288865"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} [:db/retract 17592186257022 :question/answer-fields 17592186274294] {:db/id "q-113941/selection/ma-v1/b5dcf62b933c46a3281b5d2641a372761ee2b640c98f0d5b6278f418d363ebe0/785191ef33be816e41a920a311a151a6c3016ec076d42f71db00f969ff7b6eba" :answer/id #uuid "5da6d1aa-a178-5fed-8366-37801947f8b5" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{k=1}^{n}[\\sqrt{1+\\frac{9k}{n}}\\cdot \\frac{9}{n}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-113941/selection/ma-v1/b5dcf62b933c46a3281b5d2641a372761ee2b640c98f0d5b6278f418d363ebe0/48cd51c79fcf51e4ee0d